17592186416062 1074 "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ with $x+y=4.$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=3$ satisfies $x+y=4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,3),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $x+y=4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(2,2),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\n- If $x=3,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x+y=4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(3,1),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/3d/3dec67ac3a21ca9db5967f5e650fd79d6aaadab05c900db24b7a9669a872f2f2.png)" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416063 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416051 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416063 1069 "q-213265" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416063 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416063 1071 "Let $A={1,2,4}.$ Which of the following directed graphs represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\le {x}^{2}}\n$$" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416063 1072 #uuid "480a0347-e790-59c1-bcd0-69556d5de562" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416063 1074 "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nIn our case, the nodes of the directed graph are marked by the elements of $A.$\n\n![](images/fe/fe1e7ad055ab47eca3234dfc3797a7316c554868a62cb4e46a303d369baeeaa0.png)\n\n \n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ with $y\\le {x}^{2}.$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $y\\le {x}^{2}.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,1),$ and we should add the following arrow to our directed graph:\n\n$$\n1\\to 1\n$$\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=1,2,4$ satisfy $y\\le {x}^{2}.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,1),\\,(2,2),\\,(2,4),$ and we should add the following arrows to our directed graph:\n\n$$\n2\\to 1,\\,2\\to 2,\\,2\\to 4\n$$\n\n- If $x=4,$ then the values $y=1,2,4$ satisfy $y\\le {x}^{2}.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(4,1),$ $(4,2),$ $(4,4),$ and we should add the following arrows to our directed graph:\n\n$$\n4\\to 1,\\,4\\to 2,\\,4\\to 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following digraph:\n\n![](images/e5/e5f595bac27c15ffd3adca91b9a20cefdda9abb18123d706fc50d0fc625f5454.png)" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416064 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416048 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416064 1069 "q-213261" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416064 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416064 1071 "![](images/de/ded4e8a94730585809c5534fee8d401481c919d529a67cd088e96b8797f2b237.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set ${2,5,8}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n- $(2,2)$\n- $(2,8)$\n- $(8,5)$" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416064 1072 #uuid "538c45af-5de6-56ca-85b6-7f902af26d1f" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416064 1074 "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,2)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a loop from the node $2$ to itself.\n\n- The pair $(2,8)$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $2$ and ends at the node $8.$\n\n- The pair $(8,5)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has an arrow that starts at the node $8$ and ends at the node $5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416065 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416053 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416065 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416054 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416065 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416058 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416065 1069 "q-213254" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416065 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416065 1071 "![](images/38/38df1bcf68146317ae6450e495658203a6427551d0510bd232c8cbfe5b82d386.png)\n\nExpress the relation $R⊆A\\times A$ represented by the mapping diagram above as a set of ordered pairs.\n\n$R={(w,w),(w,$\n{{field-1}}$),($\n{{field-2}}$,x),(z,$\n{{field-3}}$)}$" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416065 1072 #uuid "57a0e862-6493-53ed-a45b-3b4d586c00e7" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416065 1074 "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nOur diagram has the following arrows:\n\n- Arrows starting from $w:$\n\n- $w\\to w$ and $w\\to z,$ so the relation contains the pairs $(w,w)$ and $(w,z).$\n\n- No arrows start from $x.$\n\n- Arrows starting from $y:$\n\n- $y\\to x,$ so the relation contains the pair $(y,x).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $z:$\n\n- $z\\to x,$ so the relation contains the pair $(z,x).$\n\nTherefore, $R$ contains the following ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(w,w),(w,z),(y,x),(z,x)}\n$$" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416050 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416052 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416061 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416066 1069 "q-213253" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416066 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416066 1071 "![](images/c2/c22d8afca77121e994303cc67c6e85b065b452a5725603dec04273d1c8d427b3.png)\n\nExpress the relation $R⊆A\\times A$ represented by the mapping diagram above as a set of ordered pairs.\n\n$R={(a,b),(b,$\n{{field-1}}$),(c,a),($\n{{field-2}}$,d),($\n{{field-3}}$,b)}$" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416066 1072 #uuid "61dc2470-cb3c-5469-b0b2-8c0f8e6eb11f" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416066 1074 "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nOur diagram has the following arrows:\n\n- Arrows starting from $a:$\n\n- $a\\to b,$ so the relation contains the pair $(a,b).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $b:$\n\n- $b\\to b,$ so the relation contains the pair $(b,b).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $c:$\n\n- $c\\to a$ and $c\\to d,$ so the relation contains the pairs $(c,a)$ and $(c,d).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $d:$\n\n- $d\\to b,$ so the relation contains the pair $(d,b).$\n\nTherefore, $R$ contains the following ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(a,b),(b,b),(c,a),(c,d),(d,b)}\n$$" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416047 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416067 1069 "q-213262" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416067 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416067 1071 "![](images/4b/4b23d63f453246fb606969ba651104097fca785324d5d0fd2203e176dda19fa5.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set ${\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma }$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n- $(\\beta ,\\gamma )$\n- $(\\gamma ,\\alpha )$\n- $(\\alpha ,\\beta )$" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416067 1072 #uuid "8ba430a9-b9f3-5dc8-a610-c4d7d7129920" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416067 1074 "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair  $(\\beta ,\\gamma )$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $\\beta $ and ends at the node $\\gamma .$\n\n- The pair $(\\gamma ,\\alpha )$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $\\gamma $ and ends at the node $\\alpha .$\n\n- The pair $(\\alpha ,\\beta )$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a an arrow that starts at the node $\\alpha $ and ends at the node $\\beta .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416068 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416057 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416068 1069 "q-213188" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416068 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416068 1071 "Let $A={3,6,8}$ and $B={1,4,7}$ Which of the following is the graph representing the relation $R={(x,y)\\in A\\times B\\,:\\,x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)}?$" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416068 1072 #uuid "8c54244e-f11b-5241-a798-1237f1be29cb" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416068 1074 "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times B$ with $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2).$\n\n- If $x=3,$ then the values $y=1,7$ satisfy $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(3,1)$ and $(3,7),$ and we should plot these points on the graph.\n\n- If $x=6,$ then the value $y=4$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(6,4),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\n- If $x=8,$ then the value $y=4$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(8,4),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/42/42f68f95fe216faacbfaa051ec0a1a6cf75fe7e4598061e94f2ec6be87f9541f.png)" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416069 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416056 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416069 1069 "q-213269" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416069 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416069 1071 "Suppose we have the sets $A$ and $B,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={7,8,9,10},\\,B={0,1,2,3}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following mapping diagrams represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\in A\\times B\\,:\\,x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)}\n$$" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416069 1072 #uuid "8c72bf3c-0135-57d0-9bad-2176a6cb3b90" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416069 1074 "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nWe're given the following sets:\n\n$$\nA={7,8,9,10},\\,B={0,1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times B$ with $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$\n\n- If $x=7,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(7,1),$ and we should add the arrow $7\\to 1$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=8,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(8,2),$ and we should add the arrow $8\\to 2$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=9,$ then the values $y=0,3$ satisfy $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(9,0)$ and $(9,3),$ and we should add the arrows $9\\to 0$ and $9\\to 3$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=10,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(10,1),$ and we should add the arrow $10\\to 1$ to the mapping diagram.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/c2/c218c7a0015ad9a3b64b96bd41f42cb6fdca268d23192502bd8eec381861c866.png)" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416055 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416070 1069 "q-212114" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416070 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416070 1071 "Which of the following is the graph representing the relation $R={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<81}?$" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416070 1072 #uuid "af34d9f6-e221-5c78-a119-1fdc9bcb5c87" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416070 1074 "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set goes along the horizontal line,\n\n- the second set goes along the vertical line,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nIn our case, the plot contains all the points in ${R}^{2}$ for which \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<81.\n$$\n\nThese are the points that lie *inside* the circle of radius $9$ centered at the origin.\n\nFinally, since we have a strict inequality, the points on the circle must be excluded from the plot, which we depict by using a *dashed* curve:\n\n![](images/4e/4e08efd6af5e38ed82c0c1659088620b673afbfad5a751c07a492dd0fc4c497c.png)" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416046 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416071 1069 "q-213272" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416071 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416071 1071 "Let $A={Z,Q,R}.$ Which of the following directed graphs represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR={(X,Y)\\in {A}^{2}\\,:\\,Y⊆X}\n$$" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416071 1072 #uuid "b72e4164-0cff-5d5d-b282-9eeac80bb3aa" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416071 1074 "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nIn our case, the nodes of the directed graph are marked by the elements of $A.$\n\n![](images/98/983be4b904bbbf72f896c2e96d3691c74179c9e078494d581fb8c9b2c0c64b7f.png)\n\n \n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(X,Y)\\in {A}^{2}$ with $Y⊂X.$\n\n- If $X=Z,$ then the value $Y=Z$ satisfies $Y⊆X.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(Z,Z),$ and we should add the following arrow  to our directed graph:\n\n$$\nZ\\to Z\n$$\n\n- If $X=Q,$ then the values $Y=Z,Q$ satisfy $Y⊆X.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(Q,Z),(Q,Q),$ and we should add the following arrows to our directed graph:\n\n$$\nQ\\to Z,\\,Q\\to Q\n$$\n\n- If $X=R,$ then the values $y=Z,Q,R$ satisfy $Y⊆X.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(R,Z),$ $(R,Q),$ $(R,R),$ and we should add the following arrows to our directed graph:\n\n$$\nR\\to Z,\\,R\\to Q,\\,R\\to R\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following digraph:\n\n![](images/74/74f2f01dd9b2672f933118f6cec0bc0b83b9a0fa94b0e75536a7db9f0fe34e6b.png)" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416072 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416059 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416072 1069 "q-213167" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416072 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416072 1071 "![](images/35/35756183a7b4bf08baffb00d1f35dcc370b0e58831554daafa58d8f3d4359ab6.png)\n\nThe plot of the binary relation $S,$ defined over the set $X={2,4,6}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\n- $(2,2)$\n- $(4,4)$\n- $(6,2)$" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416072 1072 #uuid "e27df10f-2205-5c33-84a9-013636e28935" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true] [17592186416072 1074 "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,2)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(4,4)$ does not belong to the relation since the plot does not contain a point with such coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(6,2)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534251 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-039c3002-4123-5354-a54b-3c284e8cbe22" 17592186415984 "answer-05f6ce38-4ec7-5c4b-9a30-15cd62314ac4" 17592186415985 "answer-0aca0654-5a0b-5d1a-9c1a-4a631fc7caaf" 17592186415986 "answer-0c6d623c-ebdd-5e72-8f57-388e3aa09b2c" 17592186415987 "answer-0db9b435-693c-51f8-83dd-ff1f9738f23d" 17592186415988 "answer-0e527545-c8a4-5706-a526-a45e5bdba53b" 17592186415989 "answer-0ecde3ed-974d-55f9-b20d-3aefeefd4a9d" 17592186415990 "answer-107d4507-8231-5b8c-8e97-84b83cc2d7ba" 17592186415991 "answer-13272a5b-2de1-5012-9d4c-d0b6e9760b36" 175